  "body": "The depth ladder of the depth-stepping operations keeps the remainder in the last step. Some users expect equal steps.\n\nWhat happens:\n\n- The shared level law `camcraft_motion::machining2d::cut_levels` steps down by the full stepdown from Top Z.\n- The last level ends at Bottom Z exactly. The last step is the remainder.\n- Example: Top Z 20, Bottom Z 2, stepdown 5 gives the levels `[15, 10, 5, 2]`. The last step is 3 mm. The other steps are 5 mm.\n\nThe other choice:\n\n- Equal steps: the same number of levels, `ceil(span / stepdown)`, and each step is `span / count`.\n- The same example gives four steps of 4.5 mm: `[15.5, 11, 6.5, 2]`.\n- Each step stays at or below the stepdown. The load on the tool is more equal, and the last pass is not a light rub.\n\nWhere it came from: found while fixing #2004 (the ladder now starts one stepdown under Top Z). The fix kept the remainder and recorded this choice in `docs/decisions/2004-top-z-level.md`, decision 7.\n\nNotes:\n\n- This changes posted G-code of each depth-stepping operation (safety floor: `effort:high`).\n- By the project rule \"CAM choices are parameters\", equal steps is an operation parameter with a default, not a silent change of the law.\n- The callers are listed in the audit table of the PR for #2004.\n\nDone means:\n\n- A decision says which ladder is the default, and if the other is a parameter.\n- If a parameter is added: it is on each depth-stepping operation, it is undoable through `EditOperationParams`, it has an MCP field, and a test pins the levels of the two modes.\n",
